From: Stained Glass OS <ke7oxh@gmail.com>
Subject: [PATCH] ntdll, sechost, advapi32: event tracing sessions, providers and consumers

Event tracing was stubs on every side: StartTrace handed out a fixed
handle, ControlTrace and EnableTraceEx2 said yes to everything, OpenTrace
failed with ERROR_ACCESS_DENIED, ProcessTrace was ERROR_CALL_NOT_IMPLEMENTED,
providers were never enabled (EventEnabled was always FALSE, their
callbacks never called) and their events went nowhere. As documented now:

- Sessions (sechost): StartTrace makes a named session with a log file,
  real-time delivery or both, checking its properties as Windows does
  (ERROR_BAD_LENGTH, ERROR_INVALID_PARAMETER, ERROR_BAD_PATHNAME,
  ERROR_ALREADY_EXISTS; Wine's eventlog test's todos now pass);
  ControlTrace queries, updates, flushes and stops it (StopTrace,
  QueryTrace, FlushTrace, UpdateTrace go through it); QueryAllTraces lists
  the sessions; EnableTraceEx2 (and EnableTraceEx, EnableTrace) enables,
  disables and asks a provider to capture its state, with a level and
  keywords. Sessions live in a section over a file under
  %SystemRoot%\system32\LogFiles\WMI, so they outlive the process that
  started them, but not the system's start.
- Providers (ntdll): EventRegister calls the provider's callback when a
  session enables it (at once if one already has), disables it or asks
  for its state; EventEnabled and EventProviderEnabled answer from the
  level and keywords asked; EventWrite, EventWriteTransfer (activity and
  related activity) and EventWriteString write to every session that
  wants the event. Each process with providers keeps a named event the
  controllers set, waited on in the thread pool. EventActivityIdControl
  keeps the thread's activity ID (get, set, create, get-set, create-set).
  Classic providers (RegisterTraceGuids) get WMI_ENABLE_EVENTS and
  WMI_DISABLE_EVENTS with the logger handle, GetTraceLoggerHandle,
  GetTraceEnableLevel and GetTraceEnableFlags answer, and TraceEvent and
  TraceMessage write to that session.
- Consumers (sechost): OpenTrace (W and A) opens a log file written here or
  a real-time session; ProcessTrace gives each event to the
  EventRecordCallback (PROCESS_TRACE_MODE_EVENT_RECORD, with the related
  activity as extended data) or the classic EventCallback, a log file's
  header event first, calls the BufferCallback, honours a time window,
  and for real-time sessions goes on until the session stops or CloseTrace
  is called (CloseTrace then answers ERROR_CTX_CLOSE_PENDING and
  ProcessTrace ERROR_CANCELLED).
- advapi32: EnumerateTraceGuids lists the providers sessions enable;
  TraceQueryInformation answers TraceMaxLoggersQuery and TraceVersionInfo.

evntcons.h (EVENT_RECORD, EVENT_HEADER flags, PROCESS_TRACE_MODE_*) is
added; EVENT_TRACE_LOGFILE gets the SDK's BuffersRead, ProcessTraceMode,
EventRecordCallback and IsKernelTrace (Context was at the wrong offset);
MOF_FIELD, EventTraceGuid, KERNEL_LOGGER_NAME and the control codes too.
Log files are this implementation's own format: a Windows .etl file is
not read.

Gate: test/etwtrace-gate.sh (64- and 32-bit, test/etwtrace-probe.c).
Mutants SG_MUTANT_NO_EVENT_WRITE (ntdll/etw.c), SG_MUTANT_NO_SESSIONS,
SG_MUTANT_NO_DELIVERY (sechost/trace.c).
